write_failing.description:
The last {failures} attempts to write history failed. Recorded data is being held in memory and will be written once the database recovers, but it will be lost if Home Assistant restarts first — or if the buffer fills up.

The last error was: {error}

Check the database server (disk space, connection limits, and its own logs). This issue clears itself as soon as a write succeeds.

retention_failed.description:
Scribe was asked to delete data older than **{retention}** from `{table}`, and setting that policy failed with: {error}

Nothing was deleted and nothing is being deleted — the table keeps growing, and its history is intact. Retention is a TimescaleDB feature: it needs the extension installed on this database, `{table}` to be a hypertable, and the Scribe database user to own it.

Fix the cause, or clear the retention field in Scribe's options, then restart Home Assistant. This issue disappears once the policy is in place.

schema_failed.description:
Scribe reached `{host}` but failed while building its schema, with: {error}

Whatever it could not create is simply missing: on a new database that means **nothing is being recorded at all**, and on an existing one recording may be partial — some tables kept up to date, others stale or absent. The most common cause is privileges: the database user Scribe connects with needs to create tables in this database, and to own the ones it already created.

Grant it those rights (or point Scribe at a database it owns) and restart Home Assistant. This issue disappears once the schema is in place.

view_failed.description:
States are being recorded normally into `states_raw`, but the `{view}` view that reads them back could not be created: {error}

Every query, dashboard and example in the documentation goes through that view, so your history will look empty even though it is not. Nothing is lost.

This is usually a privileges problem, or another object already holding that name. Fix the cause and restart Home Assistant.
